<%#
  The console's two status glyphs, in one place.

  Locals: ready (boolean), label (what it means, for the tooltip).

  Rendered beside a module in the header nav and beside a secret that is
  already stored. Shared so the tick a developer learns in the nav is the same
  tick they meet on a field -- two hand-drawn ticks would drift, and a glyph
  that means "fine" has to look identical everywhere or it means nothing.

  aria-hidden with a title: the tooltip still appears on hover, and the glyph
  stays out of the accessibility tree so it cannot be announced alongside the
  words its caller renders for exactly that purpose.

  Measured against every ground it sits on -- the header and a card, in both
  schemes, plus the filled pill the current panel's link wears. Worst case is
  amber-600 on the light pill at 4.75:1, against the 3:1 WCAG 1.4.11 asks of a
  graphic that carries meaning.
%>
<svg aria-hidden="true"
     viewBox="0 0 24 24"
     fill="none"
     stroke="currentColor"
     stroke-width="2"
     stroke-linecap="round"
     stroke-linejoin="round"
     class="size-4 shrink-0 <%= ready ? "text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400" : "text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400" %>">
  <title><%= label %></title>
  <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"></circle>
  <% if ready %>
    <path d="M8 12.3 10.8 15 16 9.5"></path>
  <% else %>
    <path d="M9.4 9.6a2.7 2.7 0 1 1 3.5 2.6c-.6.25-.9.8-.9 1.5v.4"></path>
    <path d="M12 16.9v.1"></path>
  <% end %>
</svg>
